---
.title = "Performance",
.description = "What glowcap costs, as dated measurements you can repeat: wire-path allocation and footprint budgets the build pins, plus benchmark charts.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "performance.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

The build holds the costs it can count exactly
(allocations, copies, and the size of core state) and fails when one drifts.
The rest is one dated run on one machine, which a single command repeats.
[Beside other systems]($link.page('performance/compared')) holds the same
calls timed in Cap'n Proto RPC and in Spritely Goblins.

```=html
<!-- bench:glance GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<div class="bench-glance">
<a class="card bench-glance-card" href="#frame-ownership-and-dispatch"><strong>1</strong><span>allocation per delivered message</span></a>
<a class="card bench-glance-card" href="#codec-throughput"><strong>11.3M</strong><span>messages a second through the protocol engine, without sockets</span></a>
<a class="card bench-glance-card" href="#per-connection-footprint"><strong>1,040 B</strong><span>of protocol state for each idle connection</span></a>
<a class="card bench-glance-card" href="#encrypted-transport-round-trip"><strong>62,990</strong><span>encrypted round trips a second over 16 iroh connections on one thread</span></a>
<a class="card bench-glance-card" href="#cost-through-each-language-binding"><strong>2 ms</strong><span>from starting the Zig client to its first finished task</span></a>
<a class="card bench-glance-card" href="#persistent-actors-in-loam"><strong>2.2 µs</strong><span>to call an actor asleep in LMDB, among 2,048</span></a>
</div>
```

```=html
<!-- bench:stamp GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<p class="bench-stamp">Every figure on this page comes from one run of commit <code>124d3d251</code> on 2026-10-18: Linux aarch64, 20 logical CPUs, 122 GiB RAM, Racket v9.3 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0-52-gdcc2b2c5, and the <code>glowcap</code> archives built by Zig 0.17.0 in ReleaseFast. <code>zig build bench-site-report</code> repeats it.</p>
```

`zig build check` fails if the allocation and size budgets change, and
`zig build perf-check` fails on a coarse timing regression. The
[route to 1.0](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/ladder.md)
names the constants and tests that hold them.

The [benchmarks README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bench/README.md)
records runs too large or too machine-specific for a chart:

- [floors](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bench/README.md#floors):
  what signing, a Noise handshake, a frame copy, and a bare loopback round trip
  cost alone, and how far each glowcap call sits above the floors it pays
- calls with both runtimes spinning instead of sleeping, including an
  in-process call that makes no system call at all
- [500,000 live sessions](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bench/README.md#500000-live-sessions):
  memory per signed session on one machine

# [Codec throughput]($section.id('codec-throughput'))

`bench-codec` encodes and decodes the same small method call with
[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) and Cap'n Proto. Compare nanoseconds
per message, because MB/s also reflects that Cap'n Proto emits a larger frame. The
chart shows the small call. The bench also decodes the larger
seventy-six value `report` call into borrowed views of the frame, where the Cap'n Proto
codec reads each value in place. [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) covers the
choice.

```=html
<!-- bench:codec GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
