# [Frame ownership and dispatch]($section.id('frame-ownership-and-dispatch'))

`bench-frame-view` shows that steady delivery makes one allocation per
message, for the frame it owns. It also reads frames through `FrameView`, a
borrowed view that owns nothing, and records the sizes of the message structs.

```=html
<!-- bench:frame-view GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<table>
<thead><tr><th>metric</th><th>value</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Allocations per delivered turn</td><td>1 (build check requires <code>== 1</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frame-view projections exercised</td><td>1,000,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Argument / Message struct bytes</td><td>40 / 104</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reference / Resolution struct bytes</td><td>24 / 48</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
```

# [Encrypted transport round-trip]($section.id('encrypted-transport-round-trip'))

`bench-iroh` times one full OCapN `greet` round-trip on loopback over
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))'s keyed QUIC and over cleartext TCP as a
baseline.

The first two charts keep one call in flight. iroh's QUIC library is Rust and
runs on its own runtime, so each serial call pays a cross-thread wake that the
benchmark does not separate from encryption. The third chart runs many iroh
connections on one thread. Loopback removes propagation delay and packet
loss, so none of these values is field latency. No row here pins a core, so
where the scheduler places the two threads moves the TCP figure by two to
three times between runs. The pinned rows
[beside Cap'n Proto RPC]($link.page('performance/compared')) are the stable
measure of one cleartext call.

```=html
